    "lesson": "your lesson here"
  },
  {
    "chunk_index": 757,
    "content": "The Bond Market and Government Bonds \nGovernment bonds are auctioned out to banks and other brokers and dealers every \nMonday by the Bureau of Treasury. Depending on their terms (or tenors), these \nbonds are also called treasury bills (t-bills), treasury notes (t-notes), or treasury \nbonds (t-bonds). The resulting coupon rates and the total amount sold for these \nbonds are usually reported by news agencies on the day right after the auction.",
    "subject": "General Mathematics",
    "grade": "11",
    "unit": "your unit here",
    "topic": "your topic here",
    "lesson": "your lesson here"
  },
  {
    "chunk_index": 758,
    "content": "Since these bond transactions involve large amounts, these bonds are usually \nlimited to banks, insurance firms, and other financial institutions. The banks may then \nre-sell these bonds to its clients. \nAlthough the coupon rate for bonds is fixed, bond prices fluctuate because they are \ntraded among investors in what is called the secondary market. These prices are \ndetermined by supply and demand, the prevailing interest rates, as well as other",
